.SH COMMAND DETAILS
.SS help
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose help [<command>] | \-\-json | \-\-man | \-\-markdown
.PP
With no argument, the command overview. With a command name, that command's synopsis, flags and examples (`bullmoose <cmd> \-\-help` is the same thing). `\-\-json` dumps this entire spec — commands, subcommands, flags, examples, global options, the exit\-code table and the I/O contract notes — and is what an agent should read rather than scraping the text. `\-\-man` and `\-\-markdown` render the same spec as a man page and as Markdown; `docs/cli.md` and `man/bullmoose.1` are generated from them by `npm run \-w @bullmoose/cli gen:docs` and must never be hand\-edited.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-json
the whole command spec, machine\-readable
.TP
.B \-\-man
roff man page (→ man/bullmoose.1)
.TP
.B \-\-markdown
Markdown reference (→ docs/cli.md)
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose help mailbox
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose help \-\-json | jq \-r '.commands[].name'
.EE
what can this CLI do?
.SS login
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose login <email> [\-\-base <url>] [\-\-name <device\-name>] [\-\-password <pw>] [\-\-scopes <a,b,c>]
.PP
Authenticates to a JMAP server. With no \-\-base, the server is autodiscovered from the email domain via the _jmap._tcp SRV record / .well\-known/jmap fallback (RFC 8620 §2.2). The password comes from the prompt, $BULLMOOSE_PASSWORD, or \-\-password; it is stretched locally, used once, and never stored or sent raw. \-\-scopes sets what the minted token may do and is OPTIONAL here — this is the one command that must work before you hold any token, so omitting it takes the server default of `mail` (the six mail verbs; no contacts, calendar or vault). `login` is also the only self\-service way to WIDEN scope, because `token create` can only narrow the token it is called with.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-base <url>
JMAP server base; skip to autodiscover from the email domain
.TP
.B \-\-name <device\-name>
label the minted token (shows in `token list`)
.TP
.B \-\-password <pw>
password (else prompt or $BULLMOOSE_PASSWORD)
.TP
.B \-\-scopes <a,b,c>
scopes for the minted token; omit for the server default (mail). Vocabulary (a flat set, not an ordering): read; the mail verbs annotate, draft, move, send, delete; the bundle mail; the realms contacts, calendar, vault, files. Any write implies read.
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose login you@example.com
.EE
autodiscover the server, prompt for password
.PP
.EX
bullmoose login you@example.com \-\-base https://jmap.example.com \-\-name laptop
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose login you@example.com \-\-scopes mail,contacts,calendar,vault
.EE
widen past the default (login is the only way)
.SS discover
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose discover <email\-or\-domain>
.PP
Resolves the JMAP base for an email or domain (SRV _jmap._tcp, then .well\-known/jmap), prints the method and base, and probes the session endpoint. Read\-only; no auth.
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose discover example.com
.EE
.SS repoint
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose repoint [\-\-base <url>]
.PP
Rewrites the stored JMAP base and nothing else — the token, the default account and the local mirror are kept. With no \-\-base the server is re\-autodiscovered from your own address, which is what you want when a deployment has moved: `login`'s answer today, applied to the config `login` wrote months ago. The new base is VALIDATED with the token you already hold before anything is written, so a wrong URL leaves the old one in place; if the new server does not serve your account, it says so and changes nothing. Reach for this when a command fails with `no longer serves /.well\-known/jmap` — a 404 there means the host is gone, not down.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-base <url>
the new JMAP base, or file:// path to a bootstrap bundle; omit to autodiscover
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose repoint \-\-base https://app.bullmoose.cc
.EE
the host moved
.PP
.EX
bullmoose repoint
.EE
re\-run autodiscovery on your own address
.SS init
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose init \-\-base <url> \-\-token <token> [\-\-account <id>] [\-\-offline]
.PP
Pastes an existing token instead of logging in. \-\-base also accepts file:///path/to/bundle.json — a {base, token, accountId} bootstrap written by an operator. \-\-offline stores it without validating against the server.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-base <url>
JMAP base, or file:// path to a bootstrap bundle
.TP
.B \-\-url <url>
alias for \-\-base (accepted so a pasted bundle's own key works)
.TP
.B \-\-token <token>
a bm_… bearer token
.TP
.B \-\-account <id>
account id, if the token covers several
.TP
.B \-\-offline
store without validating
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose init \-\-base https://jmap.example.com \-\-token bm_… \-\-account t_home__a_you
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose init \-\-base file:///tmp/bootstrap.json
.EE
operator\-written bundle
.SS token
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose token create \-\-name <n> \-\-scopes <a,b,c> | list | revoke <id>
.PP
Device tokens (bm_…) are what clients authenticate with — never the login password. Scope them per device so a lost device can be revoked alone. \-\-scopes is REQUIRED: there is no default, because the shortest command should not mint the widest credential. Vocabulary is a flat set, not an ordering: the base read; the mail verbs annotate, draft, move, send, delete; the bundle `mail`, which means exactly read + those five and nothing else; and the independent realms contacts, calendar, vault, files. The one implication is that any write implies read (you cannot change what you cannot see); nothing else implies anything — delete does not imply send, and one realm never implies another. A token can only ever be narrower than the one that minted it, so to widen, run `login` again with \-\-scopes.
.PP
.B Subcommands:
.TP
.B create
mint a token (shown once) \(em \f[CR]token create \-\-name <n> \-\-scopes <a,b,c>\fR
.TP
.B list
list this account's tokens \(em \f[CR]token list\fR
.TP
.B revoke
revoke one token by id \(em \f[CR]token revoke <id>\fR
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose token create \-\-name backup \-\-scopes read
.EE
read\-only sync/archive
.PP
.EX
T=$(bullmoose token create \-\-name ci \-\-scopes read)
.EE
the token is the only thing on stdout; the chrome goes to stderr
.PP
.EX
bullmoose token create \-\-name popper \-\-scopes read,move
.EE
POP3 via popcorn
.PP
.EX
bullmoose token create \-\-name laptop \-\-scopes read,draft,send
.EE
a mail client
.PP
.EX
bullmoose token create \-\-name macbook\-contacts \-\-scopes contacts
.EE
CardDAV only
.SS accounts
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose accounts
.SS sync
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose sync [\-\-blobs <dir>] [\-\-account <sel>]
.PP
Default syncs ALL accounts: clean ones are detected in one batched round\-trip and skipped; only dirty inboxes fully sync. \-\-blobs downloads message blobs into a directory.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-blobs <dir>
also download blobs into <dir>
.TP
.B \-\-account <sel>
limit to one account
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose sync
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose sync \-\-account @example.com \-\-blobs ./blobs
.EE
.SS send
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose send \-\-to <addr>[,<addr>] \-\-subject <s> [\-\-cc ..] [\-\-bcc ..] [\-\-file <path> | \-\-body <text>]
.PP
Body comes from \-\-file, else \-\-body, else piped stdin. With \-\-expandMD html the body is treated as Markdown: rendered HTML becomes the displayed body (raw Markdown rides along as the plain\-text fallback), local images inline as cid: parts, linked files attach, and anything over \-\-linkMax is uploaded to R2 and rewritten to a signed link expiring after \-\-linkTTL days.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-to / \-\-cc / \-\-bcc <addr>
recipients (repeatable or comma\-separated)
.TP
.B \-\-subject <s>
subject line
.TP
.B \-\-file <path> / \-\-body <text>
body source (else stdin)
.TP
.B \-\-from <address>
select the sending account + identity
.TP
.B \-\-identity <id\-or\-email>
pick a specific identity
.TP
.B \-\-expandMD html|no
render Markdown to HTML (default: no)
.TP
.B \-\-linkMax <MiB>
big\-file threshold (default 4)
.TP
.B \-\-linkTTL <days>
share\-link lifetime (default 30)
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
echo "it lives" | bullmoose send \-\-to a@b.com \-\-subject "first light"
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose send \-\-to a@b.com \-\-subject Notes \-\-file notes.md \-\-expandMD html
.EE
Markdown → HTML with inline assets
.SS read
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose read [emailId] [\-\-raw] [\-\-json]
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-raw
print the raw RFC 5322 source
.TP
.B \-\-json
structured output
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose read
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose read <emailId> \-\-raw
.EE
.SS watch
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose watch [\-\-json] [\-\-exec <cmd>] [\-\-daemon | \-\-status | \-\-stop]
.PP
Holds a JMAP push channel and prints each new message. \-\-json emits NDJSON events; \-\-daemon detaches (prints a PID; logs beside the db file). \-\-exec runs a shell command once per new message: the command is handed to `sh \-c` verbatim and the message arrives in the environment as $BM_ID, $BM_ACCOUNT, $BM_FROM, $BM_SUBJECT and $BM_PREVIEW (preview truncated to 120 chars). Quote them — an inbound subject is attacker\-controlled text, and unquoted it still word\-splits inside your own command. BREAKING (was: substitution): the {id} {from} {subject} {preview} placeholders are no longer substituted — they were a shell\-injection vector, since a stranger's subject line ended up parsed by your shell. A template still containing them gets a warning on stderr. The hook is fire\-and\-forget, gets no stdin, and under \-\-json its stdout is redirected to stderr so it cannot corrupt the NDJSON stream.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-json
emit NDJSON events
.TP
.B \-\-exec <cmd>
run `sh \-c <cmd>` per new message; fields arrive as $BM_ID $BM_ACCOUNT $BM_FROM $BM_SUBJECT $BM_PREVIEW
.TP
.B \-\-daemon / \-\-status / \-\-stop
manage a detached watcher
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose watch
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose watch \-\-json \-\-exec 'notify\-send "$BM_FROM: $BM_SUBJECT"'
.EE
always quote $BM_* — the values come from whoever emailed you
.SS vacation
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose vacation on|off|status [\-\-subject <s>] [\-\-body <text>] [\-\-until <date>]
.PP
An armed auto\-responder (wait=0) with RFC 3834 suppression — once per sender per 7 days. `status` shows the current state.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-subject <s> / \-\-body <text>
the auto\-reply content
.TP
.B \-\-until <date>
auto\-disable date
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose vacation on \-\-subject "Away" \-\-body "Back Monday." \-\-until 2026\-07\-15
.EE
.SS agent
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose agent serve \-\-config <agent.json>|\-\-fleet <fleet.json> [\-\-once] | invoke <binding> \-\-email <id> | invocations [<status>] | rm <invId> | show <binding> | budget <binding> [\-\-set <µUSD>] | model <binding> [\-\-set <host>/<model>] [\-\-explore <host>/<model>]… | backfill <binding> \-\-since <date> [\-\-budget <µUSD>] [\-\-request\-floor] | enable|disable <binding>
.PP
`serve` watches the AgentInvocation queue over the same push channel as `watch`, claims pending work, and runs the binding's pipeline. Providers: mock | anthropic | openai\-compatible; API keys by env reference, never in the config (`apiKeyEnv` absent on openai\-compatible = a keyless @local endpoint like Ollama). \-\-once drains and exits (cron\-friendly). With \-\-config the config's `binding` must match the server\-side binding name (see `admin agent bind`).

Pipelines (s26 T6): the config's `pipeline` field picks the pass — `reply` (default) drafts replies in template mode; `extract` mirrors the cloud extraction pass (List\-Unsubscribe skip, cue pre\-filter, idempotence by sourceRef) and writes commitment/decision/task Annotations over Annotation/set, so the login's token needs the `annotate` scope. An extract config may carry a `modelMenu` (fallback chain, best first — config order is the ranking) and `frontier: {exploreRate}` (deterministic per\-invocation exploration, the s26 T5a assignment). The runner claims as the FREE claimant and records cost honestly in the result: 0 only for genuinely free routes (mock, keyless @local, or `free: true`), null where undetermined.

\-\-fleet (s11 T8) turns the process into a FLEET HOST: ONE login as a runtime principal serving N bindings across N accounts. Which accounts it serves is DISCOVERED from grants, not declared — each agent account grants the runtime principal claim authority (a whole\-account grant whose scopes cover `draft`), and the daemon serves whatever granted it. Adding an agent = minting a grant (no restart); revoking the grant stops that binding's claims without touching the rest. fleet.json maps binding name → {persona, model} and may declare host `capabilities` {vision, contextTokens, tools} — the daemon then skips invocations whose declared requirements it cannot satisfy. A model config describes the HOST's capability, not an agent's identity — which is why it lives in fleet.json beside the daemon rather than on the binding. That is where it lives, not a rule about where it may ever go.

`invoke` (sVOL 007) is the on\-demand trigger: it queues a pending invocation for a binding against an EXISTING message, and a runtime — your own `serve`, or the cloud runtime on its cron — picks it up over the changelog. This is how a human starts an agent on a thread rather than waiting for inbound mail. It runs on this account's own mail token, not the operator admin token. It REFUSES a binding that `admin agent disable` has turned off (the 008 kill switch): you cannot fire an agent whose off switch is pulled. `invocations` lists the queue (default: pending), and `rm` purges one — a running invocation is refused.

The DOSSIER verbs (s26 T6) read and tune ONE named binding — `<binding>` is its name or its id, resolved on the account `\-\-account` selects, exactly as everywhere else. `show` is the whole dossier: address, pipeline, enabled state, model menu (primary + explore arms), budget spent\-vs\-remaining this cycle in the same arithmetic the claim gate enforces, the work\-ledger counts, the recent invocations with their frozen cost (a null cost prints "not recorded", never a flattering $0.00), and the backfill history floor. `\-\-json` emits the whole structure as one object with `_self`/`_links` (the console projection it was read from, and the lifecycle chain when the operator plane is configured).

The verbs sit on THREE different doors and each says which. Reads and `enable`/`disable` are SESSION\-reachable on your own mail token: reads go to the console projection (`read` scope, owner\-only) and the kill switch to `AgentBinding/set`, which is gated on the `send` scope — a supervisory grant and an agent token both lack it, so neither can throw a switch. `budget \-\-set`, `model \-\-set/\-\-explore` and `backfill` are OPERATOR\-plane in THIS CLI (the provision worker's ADMIN_TOKEN, from `admin init`). That was once the only way — `AgentBinding/set` v1 wrote only `enabled` — but it is no longer the only door: `AgentBinding/set` learned budgets and the model menu on the `send` scope, so a plain mail token can now write them and this CLI has simply not adopted it yet. Migrating is tracked work, not a design position. When a door is out of reach the command says exactly that, names the call that would work, and writes nothing — it never reports success it did not get, and server refusals are printed verbatim rather than re\-worded into a guess.

Two hazards the verbs surface rather than hide. The only budget/model door is `POST /extractor`, a re\-provision\-in\-place that rewrites the binding's whole config — so `budget \-\-set` and `model \-\-set` read the current menu, arms, rate and maxTokens back first and re\-send them unchanged, and because that door also sets `enabled = 1`, re\-provisioning a DISABLED binding is refused (exit 5) unless `\-\-yes` accepts the re\-enable. And `backfill` requires `\-\-since`: the route assumes 90 days when nobody names a window, and how far into an archive an agent reads is not a default a CLI should pick. A window reaching behind the binding's history floor is refused with the server's own sentence and NOTHING is queued — moving the floor back is a tier\-1 approval, which `\-\-request\-floor` mints (and only mints: it queues no work).
.PP
.B Subcommands:
.TP
.B serve
run the homelab runtime; claims the queue (fleet: N bindings, one login, discovery from grants) \(em \f[CR]agent serve \-\-config <agent.json>|\-\-fleet <fleet.json> [\-\-once]\fR
.TP
.B invoke
queue an invocation for a binding on a message (refused if the binding is disabled) \(em \f[CR]agent invoke <binding> \-\-email <emailId> [\-\-note <text>]\fR
.TP
.B invocations
list the invocation queue (default: pending) \(em \f[CR]agent invocations [pending|running|done|failed]\fR
.TP
.B rm
purge an invocation (a running one is refused) \(em \f[CR]agent rm <invId>\fR
.TP
.B show
the agent's dossier: pipeline, state, model menu, budget spent\-vs\-remaining, queue, recent cost, floor \(em \f[CR]agent show <binding> [\-\-account <sel>] [\-\-json|\-\-ids]\fR
.TP
.B budget
read the spend envelope; \-\-set writes it through the operator\-plane re\-provision (ADMIN_TOKEN) \(em \f[CR]agent budget <binding> [\-\-set <µUSD>] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-yes]\fR
.TP
.B model
read the model menu; \-\-set swaps the primary and \-\-explore REPLACES the frontier arms (ADMIN_TOKEN) \(em \f[CR]agent model <binding> [\-\-set <host>/<model>] [\-\-explore <host>/<model>]… [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-yes]\fR
.TP
.B backfill
mint pending invocations over the archive, floor\-bounded; \-\-request\-floor asks instead (ADMIN_TOKEN) \(em \f[CR]agent backfill <binding> \-\-since <YYYY\-MM\-DD|ISO|Nd> [\-\-budget <µUSD>] [\-\-request\-floor] [\-\-dry\-run]\fR
.TP
.B enable / disable
the kill switch, over AgentBinding/set on your own session token (needs the `send` scope) \(em \f[CR]agent enable|disable <binding> [\-\-account <sel>] [\-\-dry\-run]\fR
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-config <agent.json>
serve: one\-binding agent definition (binding, persona, model{provider,baseURL,apiKeyEnv})
.TP
.B \-\-fleet <fleet.json>
serve: fleet host definition ({capabilities?, bindings: {<name>: {persona, model}}}); accounts discovered from grants
.TP
.B \-\-once
serve: drain the queue once and exit
.TP
.B \-\-email <emailId>
invoke: the message the agent acts on (required)
.TP
.B \-\-note <text>
invoke: a human note stored in the invocation context
.TP
.B \-\-set <value>
budget: the monthly cap in micro\-USD (2000000 = $2.00). model: the primary candidate as <host>/<model> — the same string `show` prints
.TP
.B \-\-explore <host>/<model>
model: a frontier arm (s26 T5a); repeatable. REPLACES the existing arms, so pass every one you want kept
.TP
.B \-\-since <date>
backfill: how far back to reach — YYYY\-MM\-DD, an ISO datetime, or <n>d. REQUIRED; there is no default window
.TP
.B \-\-budget <µUSD>
backfill: this run's own envelope, enforced at the claim gate. Omitted, the rows draw on the binding's monthly budget instead
.TP
.B \-\-request\-floor
backfill: mint the tier\-1 approval to move the history floor back INSTEAD of backfilling — it queues no work
.TP
.B \-\-yes
budget/model: accept that the re\-provision door re\-enables a binding you had disabled
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent serve \-\-config hermes.json
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent serve \-\-config hermes.json \-\-once
.EE
cron drain
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent serve \-\-fleet alpaca\-fleet.json
.EE
fleet host: one login, N granted bindings
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent invoke emily \-\-email e_9f3c…
.EE
start emily on an existing message
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent invocations
.EE
what is queued right now
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent invocations \-\-ids | xargs \-n1 bullmoose agent rm
.EE
clear the pending queue
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent serve \-\-config extractor.json \-\-once
.EE
extractor.json: {"binding":"extractor","pipeline":"extract","model":{"provider":"openai\-compatible","baseURL":"http://localhost:11434"}}
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent show extractor
.EE
the whole dossier for one agent
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent show extractor \-\-json | jq '.budget.remainingMicros'
.EE
one object, so jq reads it without a wrapper
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent disable extractor
.EE
the kill switch, on your own session token
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent budget extractor \-\-set 5000000
.EE
$5.00/month; operator plane
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent model extractor \-\-set openrouter/minimax/minimax\-m3 \-\-explore openrouter/qwen/qwen3\-30b
.EE
swap the primary and set the frontier arms in one re\-provision
.PP
.EX
bullmoose agent backfill extractor \-\-since 30d \-\-budget 500000 \-\-dry\-run
.EE
see the exact request before any invocation is minted
.SS models
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose models [\-\-host <url>] [\-\-key\-env <NAME>]
.PP
With \-\-host, asks that one endpoint (`GET <url>/v1/models`) and a dead host is an error. Without it, probes the @local ladder's default ports in order — LiteLLM :4000, Ollama :11434, vLLM :8000, llama.cpp :8080 (plus the saved @local host from `local connect`, first) — and prints what each answering host serves; a host that is down is a quiet skip, not an error. A 401/403 counts as up\-but\-keyed: pass \-\-key\-env with the name of an environment variable holding the key (a reference — the key itself is never stored or printed). Records go to stdout (one model per line; \-\-json emits NDJSON {host, base, id}); probe chrome goes to stderr.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-host <url>
ask one specific OpenAI\-compatible endpoint instead of sweeping the ladder
.TP
.B \-\-key\-env <NAME>
env var holding the host's API key (LiteLLM master key etc.) — a reference, never the key
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose models
.EE
sweep the ladder; down hosts are skipped quietly
.PP
.EX
bullmoose models \-\-host http://alpaca.local:4000 \-\-key\-env LITELLM_MASTER_KEY
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose models \-\-ids
.EE
bare model ids, one per line
.SS local
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose local setup [\-\-yes] | connect \-\-host <url> [\-\-key\-env <NAME>]
.PP
@local is a PEER, never a dependency: the product is complete without it, and nothing here installs anything without an explicit yes. `setup` (rung 1) PROBES first — LiteLLM :4000, Ollama :11434, vLLM :8000, llama.cpp :8080, the /v1/models sweep — and if a host answers it connects and stops. If a host answers but wants a key, it tells you how to connect and refuses to install a second runtime beside it. Only when nothing answers does it OFFER the managed Ollama install: the exact commands are printed first (brew / winget / the official installer, per platform), the install runs only on an explicit y — or \-\-yes, which is the same consent given ahead of time — and a starter model is pulled before connecting. Declining is exit 0: the cloud staff keeps working. `connect` (rung 2) points the CLI at ANY OpenAI\-compatible endpoint you already run — no opinions about your stack; it verifies /v1/models answers, saves the host (and the \-\-key\-env reference) in the CLI config, and reports the model list. The saved host is what `models` sweeps first and what agent configs mean by @local.
.PP
.B Subcommands:
.TP
.B setup
probe the ladder; connect if a host answers, else offer the managed Ollama install \(em \f[CR]local setup [\-\-yes]\fR
.TP
.B connect
verify and save an OpenAI\-compatible host you already run \(em \f[CR]local connect \-\-host <url> [\-\-key\-env <NAME>]\fR
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-host <url>
connect: the endpoint origin (a trailing /v1 is tolerated and stripped)
.TP
.B \-\-key\-env <NAME>
env var holding the host's API key — stored as a reference, never a value
.TP
.B \-\-yes
setup: consent to the printed install plan ahead of time (for scripts); never implied
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose local setup
.EE
detect → connect → offer → install\-with\-consent, in that order
.PP
.EX
bullmoose local connect \-\-host http://localhost:11434
.EE
rung 2: your own Ollama
.PP
.EX
bullmoose local connect \-\-host http://alpaca.local:4000 \-\-key\-env LITELLM_MASTER_KEY
.EE
.SS contacts
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose contacts import|list|show|books|create|edit|rm|export …
.PP
The full CRUD surface over the JSContact core. `import` is the idempotent bulk seed (dedup by uid); `create` makes one card without dedup; `export` is its inverse — vCard 3.0 on stdout, so `export | import` round\-trips a book with no drift. Card writes need the `contacts` scope; because any write implies read (common/027), a `contacts` token also satisfies the read verbs, so one scope covers both listing and editing cards. `books create|rename|rm` manage address books and are OWNER\-ONLY: the server refuses them on delegated (grant\-reached) access with a clean exit 4, so an agent should edit cards, not books. All write verbs take \-\-if\-state (exit 5 on a stale state) and \-\-dry\-run.
.PP
.B Subcommands:
.TP
.B import
seed from a vCard export (idempotent; dedup by uid; missing \-\-book created); reads stdin with no path, or with `\-` \(em \f[CR]contacts import [<file.vcf>|\-] [\-\-book <name\-or\-id>] [\-\-as vcard] [\-\-dry\-run]\fR
.TP
.B list
list cards \(em \f[CR]contacts list [\-\-book <name\-or\-id>] [\-n <count>] [\-\-json|\-\-ids]\fR
.TP
.B show
show one card \(em \f[CR]contacts show <cardId> [\-\-json]\fR
.TP
.B books
address\-book lifecycle; create/rename/rm are owner\-only (exit 4 on delegated access); rm of a non\-empty book needs \-\-force (else exit 5) \(em \f[CR]contacts books list | create <name> | rename <name\-or\-id> <new> | rm <name\-or\-id> [\-\-force]\fR
.TP
.B create
create card(s) from a vCard or JSON body — no dedup; reads stdin with no path, or with `\-` \(em \f[CR]contacts create [<file>|\-] [\-\-book <name\-or\-id>] [\-\-as vcard|json] [\-\-dry\-run]\fR
.TP
.B edit
replace a card's content from a vCard or JSON body (JMAP patch semantics) \(em \f[CR]contacts edit <cardId> [<file>|\-] [\-\-book <name\-or\-id>] [\-\-as vcard|json]\fR
.TP
.B rm
delete a card; resolves the target first, so a bad id is exit 3 \(em \f[CR]contacts rm <cardId> [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-if\-state <s>]\fR
.TP
.B export
the inverse of import — vCard 3.0 on stdout (or JSContact NDJSON under \-\-json) \(em \f[CR]contacts export [\-\-book <name\-or\-id>] [\-\-json|\-\-ids]\fR
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose contacts import Contacts.vcf \-\-book Personal
.EE
export from macOS Contacts: File → Export → Export vCard…
.PP
.EX
bullmoose contacts export \-\-book Personal | bullmoose contacts import \- \-\-book Backup
.EE
round\-trip a book
.PP
.EX
cat card.vcf | bullmoose contacts create \- \-\-book Personal
.EE
`\-` is explicit stdin
.PP
.EX
echo '{"name":{"full":"Ada"}}' | bullmoose contacts create \-\-as json
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose contacts export \-\-json | jq \-r .uid
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose contacts export \-\-ids | xargs \-n1 bullmoose contacts show
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose contacts books create Family \-\-if\-state "$STATE"
.EE
.SS calendar
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose calendar list | agenda | create | rename | rm | event … | export
.PP
Read verbs (`list`, `agenda`) and CRUD over the live JMAP methods. An event body may come from flags (\-\-title/\-\-start/\-\-duration/\-\-tz/\-\-all\-day/\-\-rrule), a JSON JSCalendar object, or an iCalendar VEVENT on stdin (`\-`) or a path; \-\-as forces the type. Recurrence is master\-only: `event edit` changes the whole series; single\-occurrence editing (\-\-occurrence) is not yet implemented and refuses cleanly. An \-\-rrule the server's expander cannot expand faithfully (e.g. FREQ=YEARLY;BYDAY=4TH) is rejected up front, naming the part, rather than written wrong.
.PP
.B Subcommands:
.TP
.B list
list calendars \(em \f[CR]calendar list [\-\-json] [\-\-ids]\fR
.TP
.B agenda
upcoming occurrences, recurrence\-expanded; \-\-ids yields the event ids \(em \f[CR]calendar agenda [\-\-days <n>] [\-\-json] [\-\-ids]\fR
.TP
.B create
create a calendar \(em \f[CR]calendar create <name> [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-if\-state <s>]\fR
.TP
.B rename
rename a calendar \(em \f[CR]calendar rename <id\-or\-name> <new\-name>\fR
.TP
.B rm
delete a calendar; \-\-force also removes its events \(em \f[CR]calendar rm <id\-or\-name> [\-\-force] [\-\-dry\-run]\fR
.TP
.B event create
create an event from flags, JSON, or iCal \(em \f[CR]calendar event create [<file>|\-] [\-\-calendar <id\-or\-name>] [\-\-title <t>] [\-\-start <local>] [\-\-duration <iso8601>] [\-\-tz <iana>] [\-\-all\-day] [\-\-rrule <RRULE>] [\-\-as ical|json] [\-\-dry\-run]\fR
.TP
.B event edit
edit the whole series (the master); \-\-occurrence is accepted but refuses (exit 2) \(em \f[CR]calendar event edit <id> [\-\-title …] [\-\-start …] [\-\-rrule …] [\-\-occurrence <recurrenceId>] [<patch.json>|\-] [\-\-if\-state <s>]\fR
.TP
.B event rm
delete an event \(em \f[CR]calendar event rm <id> [\-\-dry\-run]\fR
.TP
.B export
dump events as iCalendar or NDJSON JSCalendar \(em \f[CR]calendar export [\-\-ics] [\-\-calendar <id\-or\-name>] [\-\-json] [\-\-ids]\fR
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose calendar agenda \-\-days 14
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose calendar create Work
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose calendar event create \-\-title 'Standup' \-\-start 2026\-07\-08T09:00:00 \-\-duration PT15M \-\-tz America/Chicago
.EE
.PP
.EX
cat meeting.ics | bullmoose calendar event create \- \-\-calendar Work
.EE
`\-` is explicit stdin
.PP
.EX
bullmoose calendar export \-\-ics > backup.ics
.EE
open in Apple Calendar to verify
.PP
.EX
bullmoose calendar agenda \-\-ids | xargs \-n1 bullmoose calendar event rm \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.SS creds
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose creds init | set <name> | list | show <name> | rotate <name> | rm <name> | oauth <name> …
.PP
The vault stores third\-party API keys, OAuth refresh tokens and signing keys for agents. It is WRITE\-ONLY — secrets go in and are never returned (`show`/`list` are metadata only). Every credential carries the Bureau's mint\-time contract (bureau.md §5): a `\-\-kind` that gates which verbs may ever use it, and a `\-\-allow` destination binding it fails closed without. NOTHING enforces the binding, verb set or redaction yet — the Bureau proxy is a later task; `\-\-enforcement broad` records that only our code will, once it exists. `oauth` runs a browser + localhost PKCE flow and uploads only the refresh token.
.PP
.B Subcommands:
.TP
.B init
point the vault at the agent worker \(em \f[CR]creds init \-\-url <agent\-worker\-url>\fR
.TP
.B set
mint a credential with its §5 contract (else hidden prompt) \(em \f[CR]creds set <name> \-\-kind <kind> \-\-allow <origin> [\-\-header "Name: …{}…"] [\-\-scope actor] [\-\-enforcement federated|narrow|broad] [\-\-secret <s> | \-\-secret\-env VAR] [\-\-meta k=v,…]\fR
.TP
.B list
list names, kinds and destination bindings (never values) \(em \f[CR]creds list\fR
.TP
.B show
one credential's metadata — never the secret \(em \f[CR]creds show <name>\fR
.TP
.B rotate
re\-seal a new secret under the same name (refs unchanged) \(em \f[CR]creds rotate <name> [\-\-secret <s> | \-\-secret\-env VAR]\fR
.TP
.B rm
remove a credential \(em \f[CR]creds rm <name>\fR
.TP
.B oauth
PKCE flow; uploads only the refresh token \(em \f[CR]creds oauth <name> \-\-authorize\-url <u> \-\-token\-url <u> \-\-client\-id <id> [\-\-client\-secret <s>] [\-\-oauth\-scopes "a b"] [\-\-allow <origin>] [\-\-meta k=v,…] [\-\-port <n>]\fR
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-kind api\-key|oauth\-refresh|aws\-sigv4|hmac\-key
what the credential is; gates which Bureau verbs may ever use it (bureau.md §4.1)
.TP
.B \-\-allow <origin>
destination binding — the primary control; an origin (https://host) or a *.wildcard. Required on `set`: fail closed (§6)
.TP
.B \-\-header "Name: …{}…"
injection recipe; the {} is where the value goes. Header\-only, never a query param. Defaults to Authorization: Bearer {} for api\-key
.TP
.B \-\-scope actor
who may open the row; only `actor` today — `inbox`/`global` need the AAD re\-seal (§9), deferred
.TP
.B \-\-enforcement federated|narrow|broad
which §5.2 rung enforces the narrowing; `broad` (default) = only our code will, once the proxy exists
.TP
.B \-\-secret <s> / \-\-secret\-env VAR
the value, or the env var holding it (else a hidden prompt — never argv)
.TP
.B \-\-meta k=v,…
free\-form metadata stored beside the credential (also accepted on `oauth`)
.TP
.B \-\-port <n>
localhost port for the `oauth` PKCE callback (default 8976)
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
on `rm`/`rotate`: report what would happen, write nothing
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose creds set stripe \-\-kind api\-key \-\-allow https://api.stripe.com \-\-secret\-env STRIPE_KEY
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose creds set aws\-mcp \-\-kind aws\-sigv4 \-\-allow "*.amazonaws.com" \-\-enforcement narrow \-\-secret\-env AWS_SECRET
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose creds oauth gcal \-\-authorize\-url … \-\-token\-url … \-\-client\-id … \-\-port 9000
.EE
.SS log
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose log [\-n <count>] [\-\-mailbox <role\-or\-id>] [\-\-account <sel>] [\-\-json]
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-n <count>
how many (default 20)
.TP
.B \-\-mailbox <role\-or\-id>
filter by mailbox (e.g. inbox, sent)
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose log \-n 50 \-\-mailbox inbox
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose log | head \-3
.EE
exits 0 silently — a closed pipe is not an error
.PP
.EX
bullmoose log \-\-json | jq \-r .subject
.EE
NDJSON: streams, one record per line
.PP
.EX
bullmoose log \-\-ids | xargs \-n1 bullmoose show
.EE
bare ids, the xargs shape
.SS search
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose search <fts5\-query> [\-\-account <sel>] [\-\-json]
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose search "invoice NEAR quote"
.EE
.SS show
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose show <emailId> [\-\-json]
.SS mailboxes
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose mailboxes [\-\-json]
.PP
Reads the LOCAL mirror, so it shows what the last `sync` (or `mailbox` write) fetched. Use `mailbox` to create, rename, move or remove folders.
.SS mailbox
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose mailbox create <name> | rename <box> <new> | move <box> \-\-parent <box|\-> | rm <box> [\-\-force]
.PP
Folder management via Mailbox/set. A <box> is an id, a role (inbox, sent, drafts, trash, junk, archive), or a name — names are matched case\-insensitively and an ambiguous one is refused. Folders nest: \-\-parent puts a new or existing folder under another, up to the server's advertised maxMailboxDepth (10), and \-\-parent \- moves one back to the top level. Names must be unique among siblings. Role folders may be renamed but never removed, and `rm` refuses a folder that still holds mail or has children — `\-\-force` removes the mail with it, destroying any message that is in no other folder. Every verb refreshes the local mirror on success, so `bullmoose mailboxes` is current immediately without a full `sync`.
.PP
.B Subcommands:
.TP
.B create
make a folder \(em \f[CR]mailbox create <name> [\-\-parent <box>] [\-\-sort <n>]\fR
.TP
.B rename
rename a folder (roles may be renamed) \(em \f[CR]mailbox rename <box> <new\-name>\fR
.TP
.B move
reparent a folder ('\-' = top level) \(em \f[CR]mailbox move <box> \-\-parent <box|\->\fR
.TP
.B rm
remove a folder; \-\-force takes its mail too \(em \f[CR]mailbox rm <box> [\-\-force]\fR
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-parent <box>
parent folder for create/move; '\-' means top level
.TP
.B \-\-sort <n>
sortOrder for create (unsigned integer, default 0)
.TP
.B \-\-force
on rm: onDestroyRemoveEmails — remove the mail inside it too
.TP
.B \-\-if\-state <state>
refuse the write (exit 5) if the account has moved on since <state>
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
resolve the folder and report; write nothing
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose mailbox create Receipts
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose mailbox create 2026 \-\-parent Receipts
.EE
nest under an existing folder
.PP
.EX
bullmoose mailbox rename Receipts Invoices
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose mailbox move Invoices \-\-parent \-
.EE
back to the top level
.PP
.EX
bullmoose mailbox rm Invoices \-\-force
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose mailbox rm Invoices \-\-dry\-run
.EE
resolves the name, writes nothing
.PP
.EX
S=$(bullmoose mailbox create A \-\-json | jq \-r .state); bullmoose mailbox rename A B \-\-if\-state "$S"
.EE
read\-modify\-write that cannot clobber a concurrent change: exit 5 if it would
.SS flag
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose flag <id…> \-\-add <keyword> [\-\-remove <keyword>] [\-\-if\-state <s>]
.PP
Adds and removes RFC 8621 keywords on one or more messages via Email/set. Keywords are a set: \-\-add and \-\-remove take system flags ($seen, $flagged, $answered, $forwarded, $draft) or custom labels, and both are repeatable. Quote system flags — $flagged is a shell variable unquoted. This is a keywords\-only patch, so it needs the `annotate` scope alone (not `move` or `draft`). Ids come as arguments (the xargs shape) or on stdin, and stdout is the ids it changed, so verbs chain. The local mirror is reconciled on success unless \-\-no\-sync.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-add <keyword>
keyword to set (repeatable); e.g. \-\-add '$flagged'
.TP
.B \-\-remove <keyword>
keyword to clear (repeatable); e.g. \-\-remove '$seen'
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sync
skip reconciling the local mirror (batch, then sync once)
.TP
.B \-\-if\-state <state>
refuse (exit 5) if the account moved on since <state>
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose flag em_1 \-\-add '$flagged'
.EE
quote it — $flagged is a shell var
.PP
.EX
bullmoose search important \-\-ids | xargs bullmoose flag \-\-add '$flagged'
.EE
.SS seen
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose seen <id…> [\-\-unset]
.PP
Sugar for `flag \-\-add '$seen'` (or `\-\-remove` with \-\-unset). `bullmoose read` does NOT mark a message read, so this is how a script or a human clears the unread dot. Ids come as arguments or on stdin.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-unset
mark UNread instead (clear $seen)
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose log \-n 200 \-\-json | jq \-r 'select(.seen==0) | .id' | xargs bullmoose seen
.EE
.SS move
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose move <id…> \-\-role <role> | \-\-mailbox <id\-or\-name> [\-\-if\-state <s>]
.PP
REPLACES a message's mailbox set with the single named target, via an Email/set mailboxIds patch (scope: `move`). \-\-role names a seeded role folder (inbox, sent, drafts, trash, junk, archive); \-\-mailbox names any folder by id or name. Contrast `label`, which ADDS or removes one mailbox without disturbing the others — getting this wrong is the classic mail\-CLI bug. Ids come as arguments or on stdin; stdout is the ids it moved; the local mirror is reconciled unless \-\-no\-sync.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-role <role>
target a seeded role folder (archive, junk, trash, inbox, …)
.TP
.B \-\-mailbox <id\-or\-name>
target any folder by id or name
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sync
skip reconciling the local mirror
.TP
.B \-\-if\-state <state>
refuse (exit 5) if the account moved on since <state>
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
resolve the destination and report; write nothing
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose move em_1 \-\-mailbox Receipts
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose search 'from:amazon' \-\-ids | xargs bullmoose move \-\-role archive
.EE
.SS label
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose label <id…> \-\-add <mailbox> [\-\-remove <mailbox>]
.PP
JMAP's mailboxIds is a SET — a message can live in several folders — so `label` adds and removes individual mailboxes with a per\-key patch (scope: `move`), leaving the rest in place. This is what you want when `move` (which replaces the whole set) would unfile the message. A \-\-remove that would leave a message in NO mailbox is refused client\-side, naming `move`, rather than surfacing a server invalidProperties. Both flags take an id or name and are repeatable.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-add <mailbox>
mailbox (id or name) to add (repeatable)
.TP
.B \-\-remove <mailbox>
mailbox to remove; refused if it would empty the set
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sync
skip reconciling the local mirror
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose label em_1 \-\-add Receipts
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose search receipt \-\-ids | xargs bullmoose label \-\-add Receipts
.EE
.SS archive
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose archive <id…> [\-\-if\-state <s>] [\-\-dry\-run]
.PP
The most\-used triage verb: move messages into the seeded `archive` role mailbox. Sugar for `move \-\-role archive`. Ids come as arguments or on stdin; stdout is the ids archived, so it chains into the next verb.
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose archive em_1 em_2
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose search 'from:amazon' \-\-ids | xargs bullmoose archive
.EE
.SS junk
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose junk <id…>
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose search spammy \-\-ids | xargs bullmoose junk
.EE
.SS trash
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose trash <id…> [\-\-dry\-run]
.PP
Moves messages to the seeded `trash` role mailbox. This is what a human means by "delete": the message is recoverable from Trash. For a permanent, unrecoverable destroy use `rm \-\-force`.
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose search unsubscribe \-\-ids | xargs bullmoose trash \-\-dry\-run
.EE
rehearse the sweep
.PP
.EX
bullmoose search unsubscribe \-\-ids | xargs bullmoose trash
.EE
then run it
.SS rm
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose rm <id…> \-\-force  |  \-\-dry\-run
.PP
Destroys messages via Email/set destroy (scope: `delete`). This is a HARD delete: the rows are removed, the R2 blob is orphaned, there is no tombstone and NOTHING is recoverable. It is not Trash. Because of that it REFUSES without \-\-force; use \-\-dry\-run to see exactly what it would destroy first. If you meant "move to Trash", use `bullmoose trash`. `delete` is an alias for this command.
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-force
required: confirm the permanent, unrecoverable destroy
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
list what would be destroyed; destroy nothing
.TP
.B \-\-if\-state <state>
refuse (exit 5) if the account moved on since <state>
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sync
skip reconciling the local mirror
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose search 'older_than:1y' \-\-ids | xargs bullmoose rm \-\-dry\-run
.EE
rehearse first
.PP
.EX
bullmoose rm em_1 \-\-force
.EE
permanent; prefer `trash` unless you are sure
.SS delete
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose delete <id…> \-\-force  |  \-\-dry\-run
.PP
Identical to `bullmoose rm`: a hard, unrecoverable Email/set destroy that refuses without \-\-force. See `bullmoose help rm`. To move to Trash reversibly, use `bullmoose trash`.
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose search 'older_than:1y' \-\-ids | xargs bullmoose delete \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.SS blobs
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose blobs list | rm <blobId> [\-\-account <sel>] [\-\-json]
.PP
Attachments and raw messages live in R2 under a per\-account prefix. `list` is the only way to find out what is actually stored and how big it is — until it existed, nothing could answer that question while the storage was still billed. `rm` deletes ONE object and refuses (409) if it is still referenced: content\-addressed blobs are shared, so the same bytes attached to two messages are one object, and deleting it because one message is gone would break the other. It also refuses while a live share link points at the blob — revoke the link first, so the recipient gets a clear refusal rather than a link that silently starts failing. This is explicit delete only; there is no garbage\-collection sweep, deliberately (a sweep written before Files exists would delete FileNode\-backed blobs).
.PP
.B Subcommands:
.TP
.B list
objects and sizes, largest first \(em \f[CR]blobs list\fR
.TP
.B rm
delete one object; refused if referenced \(em \f[CR]blobs rm <blobId>\fR
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-account <sel>
which account, when you have more than one
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose blobs list
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose blobs rm b_9f3c… 
.EE
refused while any message or share needs it
.SS share
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose share list | revoke <shareId> [\-\-account <sel>] [\-\-json]
.PP
`bullmoose send` mints a public link for any attachment over \-\-link\-max, and those links used to be permanent for their whole TTL (up to 90 days) with no way to list or cancel them. `share list` shows every link the server still has a record of, live or revoked, with its expiry. `share revoke` is the kill switch: the link stops resolving and returns exactly the same refusal as a forged one, so nobody can probe which ids exist. Records are kept in KV and expire with the link they describe, so an expired link cleans itself up and `list` never grows without bound. Revocation is eventually consistent — allow up to a minute for it to take effect at every edge. To kill EVERY link for EVERY account at once, rotate the server's SHARE_SIGNING_KEY (see docs/DEPLOY.md); that is the break\-glass, and its blast radius is total.
.PP
.B Subcommands:
.TP
.B list
every minted link, live ones first \(em \f[CR]share list\fR
.TP
.B revoke
stop a link resolving \(em \f[CR]share revoke <shareId>\fR
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-account <sel>
which account, when you have more than one
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose share list
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose share revoke sh_4c1f…
.EE
reload the link: it now refuses
.SS identity
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose identity list | show <id> | signature <id> [\-\-text <file|\->] | add <email> | rm <id>
.PP
The addresses this account may put in From:, and the signature attached to each. An <id> is an identity id or its email address. `signature` reads the signature from \-\-text (a file, or \- for stdin), from \-\-html for the HTML alternative, or from a bare pipe; \-\-clear removes both. `send` inserts the signature itself, below the RFC 3676 "\-\- " separator, because RFC 8621 defines it as something the client inserts — a third\-party JMAP client will not apply it. `add` refuses an address that is not on one of your tenant's active domains, and `rm` refuses the account's provisioned primary.
.PP
.B Subcommands:
.TP
.B list
every send\-as address (primary first) \(em \f[CR]identity list\fR
.TP
.B show
one identity, with its signature \(em \f[CR]identity show <id\-or\-email>\fR
.TP
.B signature
set or clear the signature \(em \f[CR]identity signature <id\-or\-email> [\-\-text <file|\->] [\-\-html <file>] [\-\-clear]\fR
.TP
.B add
add a send\-as address on an active domain \(em \f[CR]identity add <email> [\-\-name <n>] [\-\-reply\-to <addr>] [\-\-bcc <addr>]\fR
.TP
.B rm
remove a send\-as address (never the primary) \(em \f[CR]identity rm <id\-or\-email>\fR
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-text <file|\->
plain\-text signature source; \- is stdin
.TP
.B \-\-html <file|\->
HTML signature source (a snippet, not a document)
.TP
.B \-\-clear
on signature: remove both signatures
.TP
.B \-\-name <n>
display name for add
.TP
.B \-\-reply\-to <addr>
Reply\-To for mail sent from this identity
.TP
.B \-\-bcc <addr>
silent Bcc for mail sent from this identity
.TP
.B \-\-if\-state <state>
refuse the write (exit 5) if the account has moved on since <state>
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
resolve the identity and report; write nothing
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose identity list
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose identity signature default \-\-text ~/.signature
.EE
.PP
.EX
printf 'Eric\\nbullmoose.cc\\n' | bullmoose identity signature default
.EE
a bare pipe is the signature
.PP
.EX
bullmoose send \-\-to you@example.com \-\-subject hi \-\-body test
.EE
the signature travels with it
.PP
.EX
bullmoose identity add hello@example.com \-\-name Sales
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose send \-\-to a@b.com \-\-identity hello@example.com \-\-subject hi \-\-body test
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose identity rm hello@example.com \-\-dry\-run
.EE
resolves the address, writes nothing
.SS admin
.B Synopsis:
.br
bullmoose admin <noun> <verb> …
.PP
Onboarding and administration. `admin init` stores the provision URL + admin token; the rest manage tenants, domains, accounts, agent bindings, tokens, and grants. A tenant id (e.g. t_home) is a slug you choose — a namespace, not a secret.

Onboarding ONE person is six of these, in order: `tenant create` (once per household/org) → `domain add` (once per domain) → `account create` → `password` → `extractor on` → optionally `byok seal`. `docs/playbooks/onboarding\-a\-second\-human.md` is that path written out, including the parts the person does themselves. Account creation is deliberately operator\-gated: there is no self\-signup, so every account on this deployment exists because someone with the admin token decided it should.

Lifecycle verbs come in two flavours. REVERSIBLE ones — `agent disable|enable`, `domain suspend|resume`, both renames — just run. IRREVERSIBLE ones — `tenant delete`, `domain delete`, `account delete`, `agent unbind` — refuse without `\-\-yes`; use `\-\-dry\-run` first to see what they would do.

`agent disable` is the kill switch: both the ingest enqueue path and the agent drain gate on the binding's `enabled` column, so disabling stops an agent being invoked at all. Invocations already queued are HELD, not cancelled — the count is printed, and they resume on `enable`.

`account delete` is a SOFT delete: it removes the delivery route (D1 row and the ingest KV key together, so mail bounces immediately) and tombstones the account so it stops authenticating, but the account's mail, calendars, contacts and blobs live in a different database and are retained. The command prints exactly what it kept.
.PP
.B Subcommands:
.TP
.B init
configure the operator endpoint \(em \f[CR]admin init \-\-url <provision\-url> \-\-token <admin\-token>\fR
.TP
.B tenant
manage tenants (namespaces) \(em \f[CR]admin tenant create <id> \-\-name <n> | list | rename <id> \-\-name <n> | delete <id> \-\-yes\fR
.TP
.B domain
wire a domain (Email Routing, SES identity, DKIM/DMARC); suspend stops mail reversibly, delete refuses while accounts remain \(em \f[CR]admin domain add <domain> \-\-tenant <t> | status <domain> | list | suspend <domain> | resume <domain> | delete <domain> \-\-yes\fR
.TP
.B account
create, rename and (soft) delete a mailbox account \(em \f[CR]admin account create <local@domain> \-\-tenant <t> [\-\-name <n>] [\-\-principal <email>] | list [\-\-tenant <t>] [\-\-include\-deleted] | rename <accountId> \-\-name <n> | delete <accountId> \-\-yes\fR
.TP
.B password
set a principal's login password \(em \f[CR]admin password <email>\fR
.TP
.B agent
bind a cloud agent runtime to a mailbox — and disable it when it misbehaves \(em \f[CR]admin agent bind <account\-email> \-\-name <binding> [\-\-sla <s>] [\-\-allow a@b,c@d] [\-\-reply\-mode send|draft] [\-\-config <file.json>] | list <account\-email> | disable <binding\-id> | enable <binding\-id> | unbind <binding\-id> \-\-yes\fR
.TP
.B token
mint operator/agent tokens for any account (\-\-scopes required; only this command may mint `admin`) \(em \f[CR]admin token create <email> \-\-name <n> \-\-scopes <a,b,c> | list [<email>] | revoke <id>\fR
.TP
.B grant
cross\-account delegation (effective rights = token ∩ grant) \(em \f[CR]admin grant create <grantee\-email> <target\-email> [\-\-scopes read,contacts] [\-\-book <id>] [\-\-expires <days>] | list [<email>] | revoke <id>\fR
.TP
.B extractor
turn the extraction pass on for ONE account — a PAID pipeline, capped ($2.00/month unless \-\-budget says otherwise) \(em \f[CR]admin extractor on <account\-email> [\-\-provider <host>] [\-\-model <slug>] [\-\-budget <micro\-USD>] [\-\-explore <host>/<model>]…\fR
.TP
.B byok
seal a tenant's OWN model\-provider key so their provider\-side guardrails apply to their agents \(em \f[CR]admin byok seal <account\-email> [\-\-provider openrouter] [\-\-allow <origin>] [\-\-name <binding>] [\-\-expires <days>] [\-\-key\-env <VAR>]\fR
.PP
.B Flags:
.TP
.B \-\-yes
confirm an irreversible verb (tenant/domain/account delete, agent unbind); nothing else needs it
.TP
.B \-\-password <pw>
on `admin password`, the new password. Prefer $BULLMOOSE_PASSWORD or the hidden prompt — a flag value lands in shell history
.TP
.B \-\-account <email>
on `agent disable|enable|unbind`, the binding's account — only needed if one binding id exists on more than one account
.TP
.B \-\-include\-deleted
on `account list`, also show tombstoned accounts (the forensic view; they are hidden by default)
.TP
.B \-\-provider <host>
on `extractor on` / `byok seal`, the HOST a model or key lives at (default openrouter)
.TP
.B \-\-model <slug>
on `extractor on`, the model id as that host spells it
.TP
.B \-\-budget <micro\-USD>
on `extractor on`, the monthly cap (2000000 = $2.00; 0 refuses every paid claim)
.TP
.B \-\-explore <host>/<model>
on `extractor on`, repeatable frontier arms added beside the primary candidate
.TP
.B \-\-key\-env <VAR>
on `byok seal`, the NAME of the env var holding the key — there is no \-\-key, because a key in argv is in your shell history
.PP
.B Examples:
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin init \-\-url https://bullmoose\-provision.<acct>.workers.dev \-\-token $ADMIN_TOKEN
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin tenant create t_home \-\-name "Home"
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin domain add example.com \-\-tenant t_home
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin account create you@example.com \-\-tenant t_home
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin agent bind editor@example.com \-\-name editor \-\-reply\-mode draft \-\-config docs/examples/editor\-emily.config.json
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin agent disable bind_9f2c1a04
.EE
the kill switch: no further invocations are enqueued or drained
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin agent list editor@example.com \-\-ids | xargs \-n1 bullmoose admin agent disable
.EE
stop every agent on one mailbox
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin domain suspend exmaple.com
.EE
mail bounces 550 immediately; `resume` puts it back, forwardTo and all
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin domain delete exmaple.com \-\-dry\-run
.EE
the typo'd domain — check before you mean it
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin account delete t_home__a_3f2a1b9c \-\-yes
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin token create hermes@example.com \-\-name hermes\-bridge \-\-scopes read,send
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin grant create partner@example.com you@example.com \-\-scopes read,contacts \-\-book <bookId> \-\-expires 365
.EE
.PP
.EX
bullmoose admin extractor on partner@example.com
.EE
the first thing a new account has to SHOW for itself; capped at $2.00/month
.PP
.EX
OR_KEY=sk\-or\-… bullmoose admin byok seal partner@example.com \-\-key\-env OR_KEY
.EE
their key, their provider\-side guardrails; write\-only, and spendable only at openrouter.ai
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-db <path>
SQLite database path (default: $BULLMOOSE_DB or ~/.bullmoose/mail.db)
.TP
.B \-\-account <sel>
account selector: accountId, address, @domain\-suffix, name substring, or 'default'. For commands that act on ONE account a selector matching several is an error, not a choice — name one. Commands that legitimately fan out (log, search, sync, watch, mailboxes) still do.
.TP
.B \-\-json
machine\-readable output: NDJSON — one complete JSON value per line — for collections, exactly one object for show\-style commands. Never a wrapping array, so `| head`, `| wc \-l` and `| jq` all stream. (`help \-\-json` dumps this whole spec.)
.TP
.B \-\-ids
print bare identifiers, one per line, and nothing else — the `| xargs \-n1` shape
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
on destructive commands (move, trash, rm/delete, mailbox rm, blobs rm, share revoke, token revoke, creds rm, contacts import, vacation on|off, admin *revoke): resolve everything, report what would happen, write nothing
.TP
.B \-\-if\-state <state>
optimistic concurrency for writes: passed to JMAP as ifInState. If the server has moved on, the write is refused with exit 5 and nothing changes. The state to pass is printed by the previous write (and is in its \-\-json output as `state`).
.TP
.B \-\-as <type>
force the input content type — vcard | ical | json | text (default: inferred from the bytes)
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
show help; `bullmoose <cmd> \-\-help` shows help for one command
.TP
.B \-\-man / \-\-markdown
render the whole spec as a man page / Markdown (used to generate the docs)
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success — the command did what it said
.TP
.B 1
generic failure — server error, quota, rate limit — nothing you can restate
.TP
.B 2
usage error — unknown flag or command, missing argument, ambiguous \-\-account
.TP
.B 3
not found — no such message, mailbox, contact, account or blob
.TP
.B 4
auth / forbidden — the token is rejected or lacks the scope
.TP
.B 5
conflict — \-\-if\-state mismatch, or a precondition like a non\-empty mailbox
.SH FILES
.TP
~/.bullmoose/mail.db
Local SQLite message log (override with \-\-db or $BULLMOOSE_DB).
